    "id": "news-1791483064370-0",
    "title": "Former Somali President Sharif Sheikh Ahmed Calls for United National Front",
    "original_title": "Sharif calls for ‘united national front to save Somalia’",
    "slug": "former-somali-president-sharif-sheikh-ahmed-calls-for-united-national-front",
    "summary": "Sharif Sheikh Ahmed warns that military mobilization against political opponents could lead to chaos or authoritarianism, urging a broad national front to resolve constitutional disputes through dialogue.",
    "content": "MOGADISHU, Somalia – Former President Sharif Sheikh Ahmed, now a prominent opposition leader, issued a statement on Thursday calling for the creation of a united national front to address Somalia's political crisis. He cautioned that the use of force and military mobilization against political rivals could push the country toward either chaos or authoritarian rule.\n\nSharif's remarks come amid escalating tensions over the constitutional mandate of Somalia's current institutions. The dispute, which has been brewing for months, centers on the legitimacy of the federal government led by President Mohamed Abdullahi Farmajo and the scheduling of elections. Sharif, who served as president from 2009 to 2012, has been a vocal critic of the current administration's handling of the situation.\n\nIn his statement, Sharif emphasized that the disagreement cannot be settled through unilateral actions or violence. Instead, he advocated for dialogue, national consensus, and strict adherence to the constitution. His call for a united front echoes similar appeals from other opposition figures and international partners who have expressed concern about Somalia's stability.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, these developments are particularly significant. Many diaspora members have family ties and financial investments in Somalia, and political instability can hinder remittances, business opportunities, and efforts to rebuild the country. The diaspora has historically played a key role in peacebuilding and development, and a united front could provide a framework for them to support a peaceful resolution.\n\nThe political standoff has already led to violent clashes in Mogadishu earlier this year, displacing thousands and raising fears of renewed conflict. International observers, including the United Nations and the African Union, have urged Somali leaders to engage in constructive dialogue to avoid further deterioration. Sharif's proposal may serve as a starting point for broader negotiations, though it remains to be seen how the government will respond.\n\nAs Somalia navigates this critical juncture, the call for a national front underscores the urgent need for inclusive and peaceful dialogue. The coming weeks will be crucial in determining whether stakeholders can come together to resolve their differences and steer the country away from instability.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/sharif-sheikh-ahmed-calls-for-united-national-front-to-save-somalia/",
    "source": "somaliguardian.com",
    "source_name": "Somalia Guardian",
    "original_url": "https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/sharif-sheikh-ahmed-calls-for-united-national-front-to-save-somalia/",
    "country": "Somalia",
    "category": "Politics",
    "published_at": "2026-10-08T18:02:49.000Z",
    "first_seen_at": "2026-10-08T18:11:04.370Z",
    "update_batch": "afternoon",
    "image_url": "https://www.somaliguardian.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/10/Sharif-Sh-Ahmed-somaliguardian-news.jpg",
    "featured": false,
