    "original_title": "Somalia: Laftagaren forces take Qansah-dhere, west of Baidoa",
    "slug": "laftagaren-forces-seize-qansah-dhere-in-bay-region",
    "summary": "Forces loyal to former Southwest leader Abdiasis Laftagaren captured the town of Qansah-dhere without resistance on Thursday, marking the first territorial loss for federal forces since a March offensive ousted him from Baidoa.",
    "content": "Forces loyal to Abdiasis Laftagaren, the former president of Somalia's Southwest state, took control of Qansah-dhere, a town in the Bay region, on Thursday. The capture occurred without armed confrontation, according to local media reports. Qansah-dhere is located approximately 90 kilometers west of Baidoa, the region's administrative capital.\n\nThe development follows a military operation launched by the Somali federal government in late March that removed Laftagaren from power in Baidoa. Since then, Laftagaren has continued to assert that he remains the region's legitimate president. His forces have now established their first foothold outside federal control, with Qansah-dhere becoming the first town to fully slip from the federal government's grip since the offensive.\n\nLaftagaren has publicly stated his intention to retake Baidoa. The takeover of Qansah-dhere appears to be a step toward that goal, though the federal government has not commented on the latest developments. The town's strategic location along key supply routes could serve as a launching point for further advances.\n\nThe political standoff between Laftagaren and the federal government has raised concerns about stability in the Southwest state, which has already faced challenges from militant groups. The lack of resistance in Qansah-dhere suggests possible shifts in local allegiances or a strategic withdrawal by federal forces.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the renewed conflict in the Bay region is a worrying sign. Many diaspora members have family ties to the area and have invested in local businesses and properties. Instability could disrupt remittances and development projects. Additionally, the diaspora often plays a role in mediating political disputes, and this latest flare-up may prompt calls for dialogue to prevent a wider confrontation.\n\nObservers are closely monitoring whether the federal government will respond militarily or pursue negotiations. The situation remains fluid, and further updates are expected as events unfold.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somalia-laftagaren-forces-capture-qansah-dhere-baidoa/",
    "source": "somaliguardian.com",
    "source_name": "Somalia Guardian",
