    "title": "Storm in Bari Region Devastates Village, Leaving Hundreds Without Livelihoods",
    "original_title": "Storm leaves hundreds in Bari village bereft of crucial livelihoods",
    "slug": "storm-in-bari-region-devastates-village-leaving-hundreds-without-livelihoods",
    "summary": "A powerful storm hit El-Gal village in Somalia's Bari region on September 7, killing people and livestock, destroying homes, and worsening conditions for families already reeling from drought.",
    "content": "A severe storm struck the village of El-Gal in the Qandala district of Somalia's Bari region on September 7, causing loss of life and widespread destruction. The heavy rainfall, flash flooding, and strong winds killed both people and livestock, demolished hundreds of homes, and crippled the livelihoods of already vulnerable families. The disaster has left hundreds of households struggling to secure even one meal a day.\n\nThe storm's impact is particularly acute because the area had recently endured a prolonged drought. Many families had not yet recovered from the drought's effects when the storm hit, compounding their hardship. Among the victims was the mother of 18-year-old Abdimudalib Ahmed Mohamed, who died when their home collapsed. Abdimudalib now cares for his eight younger siblings, and the family is among many in the village facing an uncertain future.\n\nLocal livelihoods, which depend heavily on livestock and small-scale farming, have been severely disrupted. The loss of animals and damage to infrastructure have left families without their usual sources of income. Recovery efforts are complicated by the remote location of El-Gal and the limited resources available to local authorities.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the news from El-Gal is a stark reminder of the vulnerability of rural communities back home. Many diaspora members have relatives in the Bari region and often send remittances to support them. The destruction of homes and livelihoods means that families will need even more assistance to survive and rebuild. Diaspora organizations and individuals may feel compelled to increase their support, but the scale of the disaster also highlights the need for longer-term solutions to strengthen resilience against natural disasters.\n\nAs the community begins to assess the full extent of the damage, the immediate priority is providing emergency relief such as food, shelter, and medical aid. However, the underlying issues of climate vulnerability and poverty will require sustained attention. The Somali diaspora can play a vital role in both short-term relief and long-term recovery efforts.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Ergo here: https://radioergo.org/en/2026/10/storm-leaves-hundreds-in-bari-village-bereft-of-crucial-livelihoods/",
    "source": "radioergo.org",
